Complexity
- Time: \(O(n)\) worst case, because every node might be visited.
- Auxiliary space: \(O(h)\) for the recursion stack.
This works correctly but doesn't exploit the BST property.

Approach 2 — Brute Force: Iterative BFS
Idea
Use a queue to explore the tree level by level.

At each node:
1. If its value matches, return that node.
2. Otherwise, enqueue its children.
3. If the queue becomes empty, return null.
BFS also ignores the BST ordering, so it may visit many unnecessary nodes.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {

        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

            TreeNode curr = queue.poll();

            if (curr.val == val) {
                return curr;
            }

            if (curr.left != null) {
                queue.offer(curr.left);
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                queue.offer(curr.right);
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}


