Complexity
- Time: \(O(n)\) worst case.
- Auxiliary space: \(O(w)\), where \(w\) is the maximum width of the tree; worst case \(O(n)\).
This is valid but performs more work than necessary for a BST.

Approach 3 — Recursive BST Search (Your Code)
This is the standard recursive solution and is much more efficient than searching the entire tree.
Idea
Use the BST property to choose exactly one subtree at each step.
             4
            / \
           2   7


If the target is 2:
2 < 4


So search only the left subtree.

If the target is 7:
7 > 4


So search only the right subtree.

We don't need a queue, a list, or a separate visited structure.
Your code
class Solution {
    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {

        if (root == null || root.val == val) {
            return root;
        }

        if (val > root.val) {
            return searchBST(root.right, val);
        }

        return searchBST(root.left, val);
    }
}


Your code is correct and is the standard optimal recursive solution.
Understanding each condition
Base case:
if (root == null || root.val == val) {
    return root;
}


There are two possibilities:
- root == null: The target does not exist along this search path. Return null.
- root.val == val: We found the target. Return the actual node and its subtree.
If the target is larger:
if (val > root.val) {
    return searchBST(root.right, val);
}


All values in the left subtree are smaller, so searching left cannot find the target.

Otherwise:
return searchBST(root.left, val);


The target is smaller than the current node, so we search left.

