Notice that we visited only three positions, rather than traversing the entire tree.
Complexity
Let \(h\) be the height of the BST.
- Time: \(O(h)\).
- Auxiliary space: \(O(h)\) for recursion.
For a balanced BST:
- Time: \(O(\log n)\)
- Auxiliary space: \(O(\log n)\)
For a highly skewed BST:
- Time: \(O(n)\)
- Auxiliary space: \(O(n)\)

Approach 4 — Iterative BST Search (Optimal Auxiliary Space)
This approach uses the same BST logic as your code but replaces recursion with a loop.
Idea
Start at the root and repeatedly move:
Target == current value → return current node
Target < current value  → move left
Target > current value  → move right


If the current node becomes null, return null.
Code
class Solution {

    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {

        TreeNode curr = root;

        while (curr != null) {

            if (curr.val == val) {
                return curr;
            }

            if (val < curr.val) {
                curr = curr.left;
            } else {
                curr = curr.right;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}


