Complexity
- Time: \(O(h)\).
- Auxiliary space: \(O(1)\).
For a balanced BST, time is \(O(\log n)\). For a skewed BST, worst-case time is \(O(n)\).

This is the most space-efficient approach among the methods covered.

Comparison of All Approaches
Approach	Uses BST property?	Time	Auxiliary space
1. Recursive DFS	No	\(O(n)\) worst	\(O(h)\)
2. Iterative BFS	No	\(O(n)\) worst	\(O(w)\)
3. Recursive BST search — your code	Yes	\(O(h)\)	\(O(h)\)
4. Iterative BST search	Yes	\(O(h)\)	\(O(1)\)

Here:
- \(n\) = number of nodes.
- \(h\) = height of the tree.
- \(w\) = maximum width of the tree.
Which approach should you remember?
For interviews, learn these two:

Recursive BST search: easiest to understand and explain.

Iterative BST search: same performance in time, with \(O(1)\) auxiliary space.

Your recursive code is already optimal in time because it follows only one root-to-leaf search path. The iterative version improves the auxiliary space usage.

Important Edge Cases
1. Empty tree
root = null
val = 5


Output:
null


2. Target is the root
    4
   / \
  2   7


