Return the entire original tree rooted at 4.
3. Target is a leaf
    4
   / \
  2   7


Search for 7.

Output:
7


4. Target is smaller than every node
The search repeatedly moves left until it finds the target or reaches null.
5. Target is larger than every node
The search repeatedly moves right until it finds the target or reaches null.

Final Interview Pattern
When you see Search in a Binary Search Tree, remember:
             Current node
                  |
          Is target equal?
             /       \
           Yes        No
            ↓          ↓
       Return node   Compare values
                      /       \
              Target smaller  Target larger
                    ↓              ↓
               Go left          Go right


The reusable template:
class Solution {
    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {

        while (root != null && root.val != val) {
            if (val < root.val) {
                root = root.left;
            } else {
                root = root.right;
            }
        }

        return root;
    }
}


Memory trick: In a BST, every comparison tells you which entire subtree you can ignore. That's why the search takes \(O(h)\) time rather than necessarily visiting all \(n\) nodes.
